<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Subhas Chandra Bose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Netaji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Subhas Chandra Bose was an Indian nationalist leader, twice president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. Seeking foreign help against British rule, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and then Japanese-held Asia, where in 1943 he proclaimed the Provisional Government of Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (Azad Hind) and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>. He died after a plane crash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose.jpg" alt="Subhas Chandra Bose" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Netaji Research Bureau, Calcutta) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Subhas_Chandra_Bose_NRB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Subhas Chandra Bose was born on 23 January 1897 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>, then part of the Bengal Presidency, the ninth child and sixth son of Prabhabati and Janakinath Bose. His father was a successful lawyer, loyal to the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a> examination, Bose passed the first examination with distinction but declined the final one, citing nationalism as the higher calling. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in July 1921, he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> the same afternoon. They differed on means, since Gandhi held non-violence non-negotiable, and Gandhi sent him to C. R. Das, who launched him into nationalist politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bose worked within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> for nearly 20 years while trying to change its course. In the mid-1930s he travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, meeting politicians including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>. In 1938 he became Congress president, standing for unqualified Swaraj, including force against the British, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> opposed him. He was re-elected in 1939 over Gandhi's preferred candidate Pattabhi Sitaramayya, but the Congress Working Committee turned against him and he resigned.</p><p>In January 1941 he escaped from British surveillance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. There he founded the Free India Centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and created the Indian Legion from Indian prisoners of war. In February 1943 he boarded the submarine U-180, and off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> was transferred to the Japanese I-29. With Japanese support he took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>, and a Provisional Government of Free India was declared on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>. The Japanese thought him militarily unskilled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bose died on 18 August 1945 from third-degree burns after his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplane</a> crashed in Japanese-ruled Taiwan. Many of his supporters, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, refused to believe the fact or the circumstances, and conspiracy theories appeared within hours. The British charged 300 INA officers with treason, but backed down in the face of opposition from the Congress.</p><p>He is a hero to many Indians, and the title Netaji, Respected Leader, was first applied to him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in early 1942. His legacy is also contested. He preferred a synthesis of socialism and fascism, believed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> needed a strong central government with dictatorial powers for some years, and in 1939 refused to support a Congress motion offering Jewish refugees refuge in India.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Subhas Chandra Bose” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Subhas Chandra Bose</b> led an Indian National Army alongside Japan, and the Bengal famine of 1943 discredited the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1929 the Congress, meeting at Lahore under Jawaharlal Nehru, made purna swaraj, complete independence, its goal, and in the 1930s Nehru, <b>Subhas Chandra Bose</b> and the Congress Socialists linked independence with land reform and planned industry.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>In modern times Swami Vivekananda, <b>Netaji</b> Subhas Chandra Bose, Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Mahatma Gandhi and many others used it to help inspire the Indian independence movement.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its tune was used with Hindi words in the 2004 film <b>Netaji</b> Subhas Chandra Bose: The Forgotten Hero, and Amitabh Bachchan sang it in the 2012 film Kahaani.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ekla-chalo-re">Ekla Chalo Re</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Subhas Chandra Bose</b> chose it as the national anthem while he was in Germany, and on 11 September 1942 the Hamburg Radio Symphony Orchestra played it as India's anthem at the founding of the German-Indian Society.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li><li><blockquote>Asked by Congress President <b>Subhas Chandra Bose</b>, Tagore replied that the core of the poem was a hymn to Durga, fitting in the novel but not in a parliament meant for all religious groups.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Taihoku, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.45,85.87&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Cuttack on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6863 1407 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Subhas Chandra Bose"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7313 1677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7313 1677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.45,85.87&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
